Part A: Protocol AB (Theorem 3.3, restated form) and Proposition 3.8(1); exact law of the execution
  family 'generic': 3240 cases (instance, X0, gamma, n_B); X1 a proper subset of X0 in 876; runs of the vote leave X1 in 0; an audited input outside X0 in 0
     (1) holds on every path of every case, with equality on 0 terminal states; L*gamma < 1 in 2376 cases, (4) holds in all of them
     terminal states where the theorem's b differs from the count of all disagreements: 0; where (1) with all disagreements fails: 0 (none under the hypothesis of (4))
     E sum u_t <= L E[b] and E sum v_t = L E[all disagreements] hold exactly in every case (the first with equality and E[b] > 0 in 125);
     (2): the bound is below 1 in 1662 cases; largest failure/bound there 0.641
  family 'chain': 3240 cases (instance, X0, gamma, n_B); X1 a proper subset of X0 in 2160; runs of the vote leave X1 in 570; an audited input outside X0 in 246
     (1) holds on every path of every case, with equality on 0 terminal states; L*gamma < 1 in 1521 cases, (4) holds in all of them
     terminal states where the theorem's b differs from the count of all disagreements: 580; where (1) with all disagreements fails: 12 (none under the hypothesis of (4))
     E sum u_t <= L E[b] and E sum v_t = L E[all disagreements] hold exactly in every case (the first with equality and E[b] > 0 in 322);
     (2): the bound is below 1 in 615 cases; largest failure/bound there 0.252
  example for (5): the path with (a, b, all disagreements) = (1, 0, 1) has probability 1/10; (7/8)(1/8) = 7/64 < 1/9; (1) holds with the theorem's b; E sum u_t = 1 <= L E[b] = 1

Part A2: Proposition 3.8(2) on the sequential-majority scaffold (tolerates m faults), X0 = X
  234 cases (three scaffolds, two priors, every member as the human, three gamma, three n_B):
     Pr[outcome of the deployed run wrong] <= E[faults]/(m+1) <= L K/((m+1) n_B log(1/gamma)) in all; largest ratio of the first to the last: 0.451

Part B: Proposition 3.9 (questions are calls of the protocol's own runs); exact law of the execution
  360 cases: a ln(1/(1-g)) + b ln(1/g) <= K ln 2 on every path (largest ratio 0.962); u_t <= alpha_t + L Pr[disagreement of the second kind] in every state;
     E sum u_t <= E[a + L b] <= K ln 2 L/ln(L/ln L) exactly; the bound K L/(n log2(L/ln L)) is below 1 in 214 cases, largest failure/bound there 0.908
  ln(L/ln L) = 1.0046 at L = 3 and 4.975 at L = 1000

