Part E: Proposition 4.6 on toy machines (programs = binary strings of length <= 2, weights 2^(-2|p|-1))
  40 machines, 7 programs each (280 pairs of machine and task law): the bound holds in all; it is below 1 in 79, largest failure/bound there 0.563
     sum over rounds of E sum_z xi(z) beta(z) <= L K ln 2 (largest ratio 0.781); the two steps of the proof hold in every state
  weights: sum over l <= 60 of 2^l 2^(-2l-1) = 1.0

Part F: Proposition 5.6
  (b) ten triples (m, B, N0), every member, every z, every rule with at most m replacements (answers 0, 1 or silence):
      the outcome is the human system's, the run has at most B(m+1)+m calls, and m+1 replacements can change the outcome
  (a) 600 pairs (f_0, f_i) on 150 random scaffolds: in the 1166 cases (pair, z) with different outcomes, the least
      number of replacements that turns the outcome of f_i into that of f_0 is at most the number c of calls of the run
      of f_0 in D_i (smaller in 277).  The tolerance level of f_i is at least 1 for 0 of these pairs, so the
      consequences c >= level + 1 and Pr[outcomes differ] <= E[c]/(level+1) are tested with level = 0 here and with
      level = m >= 1 on the tolerant systems of the next line
      18 tolerant systems (sequential majority, a random function of the block values as outcome): 99 cases with
      different outcomes, each with at least m+1 calls of the run of f_0 in the patch; sum_i Delta_i <= L/(m+1)

