Part I: Remark 1.11 (a protocol without coins)
  300 random protocols without coins: f' has the transcript of f_0; the outcomes differ exactly when the run of f_0 leaves S (probability > 0 in 300); Fail(f_0) + Fail(f') >= that probability for every deployed system tried

Part J: Theorem 4.1(3), the protocol with n questions for N shifts
  for N <= 6 and every n <= N the least success probability over shifts and members is exactly 1/2 + n/(2N);
  so ceil(2aN) questions give the advantage a, and a mixture of the protocols with floor(2aN) and ceil(2aN)
  questions gives it with 2aN questions in expectation

